## Frequently asked follow-ups

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| *Can I keep the `font-faces` block in the JSON and just ignore it?* | The GL JS validator will flag it as an unknown property. It will be stripped out, so it has no effect. |
| *What if I need both local fonts **and** some remote glyphs (e.g., a brand font plus Noto for other scripts)?* | You can **omit `glyphs`** and load **all** fonts via `@font-face`. The browser will handle the fallback list. If you want to keep a remote glyph server for a subset, you can **still omit `glyphs`** – GL JS will never request glyph PBFs when the field is empty. |
| *Is there a way to make GL JS use `font-faces` in the future?* | There is an open discussion on the GL JS repo (issue [#6637](https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/issues/6637)). Until it lands, the two SDKs remain divergent. |
| *Do I need to set `text-font` to the exact file name (`MyBrand-Regular.ttf`) or the folder name?* | Use the **font-family name** you declared in `@font-face` (or the folder name when

Below is a step-by-step recipe for adding a **hillshade** layer that is driven by a **raster-DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything you need is in the code snippets, but the explanation that follows each block tells you why it’s there and what you can tweak.

---

## 1. Prerequisites

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 1.15** (or any recent 2.x/3.x version) | Hillshade works with the `raster-dem` source type that was added in v1.13. |
| A **DEM raster** (GeoTIFF, Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF, or any raster that MapLibre can read) served with CORS enabled (or proxied). | The DEM provides elevation values that the hillshade renderer turns into light/shadow. |
| (Optional) **Terrain** source if you also want 3-D terrain. | Hillshade can be used together with terrain, but they are independent. |

---

## 2. Add the DEM source to the style

```js
// 1️⃣  Create the map (basic example)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [8.54, 47.38],          // Zürich, for example
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣  When the style is loaded, add the DEM source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('my-dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    // URL can be a single tile URL template or a TileJSON endpoint
    // Example using a public Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF (CORS enabled):
    url: 'https://storage.googleapis.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
    // If you have a TileJSON endpoint, use `tiles: [...]` instead of `url`.
    tileSize: 256,               // default, keep it unless your source uses 512
    // Optional: tell MapLibre the DEM’s vertical exaggeration (default 1)
    // maxzoom: 14,               // highest zoom you have tiles for
    // attribution: '© My DEM provider'
  });
});
```

### What the fields mean

| Property | Required? | Description |
|----------|-----------|-------------|
| `type` | ✅ | Must be `"raster-dem"` for hillshade. |
| `url` | ✅ (or `tiles`) | Tile URL template (`{z}/{x}/{y}`) that returns a DEM tile. |
| `tileSize` | optional | 256 (default) or 512 – must match the source tiles. |
| `maxzoom` | optional | Highest zoom level you have data for; helps the renderer pick the right resolution. |
| `attribution` | optional | Legal attribution for the DEM data. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a TileJSON endpoint (e.g. from a TileServer), you can use `tiles: ['https://myserver/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png']` instead of `url`.

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
// 3️⃣  Add the hillshade layer *after* the DEM source exists
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',
  // The source-layer is not needed for raster-dem sources.
  // Paint properties control the visual appearance:
  paint: {
    // Light direction (azimuth in degrees, altitude in degrees)
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // NW light
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map', // keep direction fixed to map north
    // Optional colour tweaks:
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // lower = flatter shading
    'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
    'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
    'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
  }
});
```

### Paint properties you can tune

| Property | Default | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | `315` | `0-360` | Sun azimuth (clockwise from north). |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"viewport"` | `"map"` or `"viewport"` | `"map"` keeps the sun fixed to geographic north; `"viewport"` makes it rotate with the map. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | `0.5` | `0-1` (or >1 for dramatic effect) | Scales the slope-derived shading. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | `"#000000"` | any CSS color | Dark side colour. |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | `"#ffffff"` | any CSS color | Bright side colour. |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | `"#777777"` | any CSS color | Mid-tone accent (helps see subtle relief). |

---

## 4. (Optional) Combine with 3-D terrain

If you also want a 3-D terrain surface, add a `terrain` source **after** the DEM source:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

The hillshade layer will then be rendered **under** the terrain surface, giving a realistic shaded relief that matches the 3-D mesh.

---

## 5. Full minimal example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre Hillshade Demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [8.54, 47.38],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://storage.googleapis.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256
    });

    // Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5
      }
    });

    // Optional 3-D terrain
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser (or serve it via a local web server) and you’ll see a shaded relief map over the base style.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** (just the base map) | DEM source not added *before* the layer, or wrong source name. | Verify `map.addSource('my-dem', …)` runs before `map.addLayer({ source: 'my-dem', … })`. |
| **Grey/blank tiles** | DEM tiles not reachable (CORS, 404, or wrong URL). | Open the tile URL directly in the browser; check network console for CORS errors. Use a proxy or enable CORS on the server. |
| **Very blocky shading** | Using a low-resolution DEM (e.g. 256-px tiles at high zoom) or `tileSize: 512` mismatch. | Match `tileSize` to the source tiles; use higher-resolution DEM if available. |
| **Shading looks “flat”** | `hillshade-exaggeration` too low. | Increase to `0.8`-`1.0` or higher for dramatic effect. |
| **CJK or other scripts disappear** (unrelated to hillshade) | Not a font issue here – ignore unless you also have label problems. | Use the local-font fallback or a glyph server as described in the *maplibre-fonts-glyphs* skill. |
| **Terrain and hillshade don’t line up** | DEM source used for terrain has a different projection/extent than the hillshade source. | Use the **same** DEM source for both `setTerrain` and the hillshade layer. |

---

## 7. Where to go next

* **Custom DEM tiles** – generate your own Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFFs with `gdal_translate` and `gdaladdo`.  
* **Dynamic lighting** – change `hillshade-illumination-direction` on the fly (e.g., with a UI slider) to simulate sun movement.  
* **Multiple DEM sources** – you can stack hillshade layers from different resolutions (low-zoom global DEM + high-zoom local DEM) and control their `minzoom`/`maxzoom` to improve performance.  

That’s it! With the source and layer added as shown, your MapLibre map will render a smooth, configurable hillshade overlay derived from any raster-DEM you provide. Happy mapping!
